# Contact form — set these in Vercel (Project → Settings → Environment Variables).
# Copy to .env for local testing. .env is gitignored; never commit real keys.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PHASE 1 — launch now, zero DNS work
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Resend gives every account a shared sender, onboarding@resend.dev, that works
# WITHOUT verifying a domain. The catch is that it can only deliver to the
# address you signed up with — which is exactly what this form needs, since it
# only ever sends to one inbox.
#
# So: sign up for Resend with your Gmail, and use it as CONTACT_TO. No DKIM, no
# SPF, no touching the existing email-forwarding records. Reply-To still carries
# the visitor's address, so replying from Gmail reaches them directly.
#
#   RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxx
#   CONTACT_TO=yourname@gmail.com
#   CONTACT_FROM=Thread <onboarding@resend.dev>
#
# Check spam the first time — a shared sender domain sometimes lands there
# until you mark it as safe.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PHASE 2 — later, once you want mail from your own domain
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Verify threadhawaii.com in Resend, then change CONTACT_FROM. Nothing else
# changes — no code, no redeploy beyond the env var.
#
#   CONTACT_FROM=Thread <hello@threadhawaii.com>
#
# ⚠️ When you verify, keep the SPF record Resend asks for on the
# send.threadhawaii.com subdomain. There is already an SPF record at the root
# for Namecheap's forwarding, and a domain can only have one — a second breaks
# both. See LAUNCH-CHECKLIST.md section 6.

# From the Resend dashboard. Starts with re_
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Where system-map requests land. Your Gmail in phase 1.
CONTACT_TO=

# Phase 1: Thread <onboarding@resend.dev>
# Phase 2: Thread <hello@threadhawaii.com>
CONTACT_FROM=Thread <onboarding@resend.dev>
